      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Bay%2C_New_South_Wales"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Elizabeth Bay House, built 1835‑1839, once called “the finest house in the colony,” now showcases 19th‑century interiors.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.sydney.com/destinations/sydney/sydney-city/potts-point-and-woolloomooloo/attractions/elizabeth-bay-house"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Alexander Macleay, a renowned gentleman scientist, received the land in 1828 and commissioned the Regency‑style house.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.experiencesydneyaustralia.com/visitor-information/historic-buildings/elizabeth-bay-house/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area originally featured over 21 hectares of gardens with terraces, orchards, and exotic plants surrounding the house.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.experiencesydneyaustralia.com/visitor-information/historic-buildings/elizabeth-bay-house/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Elizabeth Bay sits on Sydney Harbour, separated from Rushcutters Bay by Macleay Point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
